            "function": {
              "name": "edit",
              "arguments": "{\"path\": \"/home/jakubs/repositories/books/romantasy-book-1/.romantic-fantasy-writer/stories/001/chapters/013/draft.md\", \"old_str\": \"\\\"Magical accelerant does not burn the way natural fire burns.\\\" Her voice was flat. She let it be flat. The flatness was the only architecture she had left. \\\"It does not spread. It arrives. Everywhere at once. The Graymarket was burning in ninety seconds.\\\"\\n\\nThrough the bond she felt Lirien's presence — not reaction, not judgment, not the pulling-away she had braced for like bracing for a wall to give. Just presence. The specific, deliberate quality of a person holding their position.\\n\\n\\\"The smell was the first thing. Plaster dust and pine resin — the market stalls burning, the resin popping in the heat. And underneath it, something sweet. I did not know what the sweetness was. Not immediately.\\\" Her hands pressed the stone so hard her knuckles ached. \\\"Rendered fat. That is what the sweet smell is. When the fire is hot enough.\\\"\\n\\nShe opened her eyes. She still could not look at Lirien. She looked at her hands instead — the hands that had not thrown the torch, had not spoken the Shaper's words, had not struck the accelerant, but had made the decision that caused all of those things.\\n\\n\\\"The families in the Graymarket had sheltered in the district temple. Stone construction. Good walls — I had surveyed them myself, months before, when we mapped defensive positions. Those walls were rated for conventional fire.\\\" She heard the crack in her own voice and could not prevent it. \\\"Not for accelerant. The temple held forty seconds longer than the surrounding structures. Then it did not hold.\\\"\", \"new_str\": \"\\\"Magical accelerant does not burn the way natural fire burns.\\\" Her voice was flat. She let it be flat. The flatness was the only architecture she had left. \\\"It does not spread. It arrives. Everywhere at once. The Graymarket was burning in ninety seconds.\\\"\\n\\nThrough the bond she felt Lirien's presence — not reaction, not judgment, not the pulling-away she had braced for like bracing for a wall to give. Just presence. The specific, deliberate quality of a person holding their position.\\n\\n\\\"The smell was the first thing. Plaster dust and pine resin — the market stalls burning, the resin popping in the heat. And underneath it, something sweet. I did not know what the sweetness was. Not immediately.\\\" Her hands pressed the stone so hard her knuckles ached. \\\"Rendered fat. That is what the sweet smell is. When the fire is hot enough.\\\"\\n\\nShe breathed. The air on the granite shelf was cold and clean and smelled like birch bark and damp earth and nothing else. She held it in her lungs the way she held the stone beneath her palms — as proof that this was now and not then, that the air did not carry what it had carried six years ago.\\n\\n\\\"The sound was the second thing.\\\" Her voice had dropped. She did not raise it. \\\"Accelerant fire does not roar. It hisses. A sustained, pressurized sound — like steam, like something being forced through a space too small for it. The hissing was the fire eating through the market stalls and the residential blocks and the district's western gate where the evacuation was still underway. Thirty-eight people were in the gate passage when the fire reached it. The gate was stone but the doors were ironwood and ironwood burns.\\\"\\n\\nShe opened her eyes. She still could not look at Lirien. She looked at her hands instead — the hands that had not thrown the torch, had not spoken the Shaper's words, had not struck the accelerant, but had made the decision that caused all of those things.\\n\\n\\\"The families in the Graymarket had sheltered in the district temple. Stone construction. Good walls — I had surveyed them myself, months before, when we mapped defensive positions. Those walls were rated for conventional fire.\\\" She heard the crack in her own voice and could not prevent it. \\\"Not for accelerant. The temple held forty seconds longer than the surrounding structures. Then it did not hold.\\\"\\n\\nShe heard it sometimes. Still. In dreams and in the moments between waking and sleep when the body forgot to defend itself. Not screaming — the fire was too fast for screaming. A sound like pressure releasing. Like stone groaning under a load it was never designed to carry. She did not tell Lirien this. She did not need to. The bond was carrying the memory's residue — not the sound itself but the shape of it in her body, the way it lived in her jaw and her shoulders and the backs of her hands, a tension that six years had not dissolved because the body did not forget the things the mind would not permit it to process.\"}"
            }
          }
        ],
        "parsed": null
      }
