              "arguments": "{\"new_str\": \"Two paces. Close enough to see the attention in Lirien's eyes — not the color, because the dark had taken color, but the focus. The way Lirien held Kaelith's gaze with the steadiness of a person who had decided not to look away.\\n\\nKaelith stopped. The bond between them was doing something she had no tactical framework for — transmitting not information but intensity, the raw weight of proximity amplified by mutual awareness until the air between them felt pressurized, load-bearing, as though the space itself had structural properties and the structure was being tested.\\n\\nOne pace. Lirien's breathing had changed. Kaelith could hear it and feel it — the double register of the bond, the sound arriving through air and the sensation arriving through resonance simultaneously, each confirming the other, neither sufficient alone.\\n\\n\\\"This is not survival,\\\" Kaelith said. Her voice was low. Rough. The formality had stripped away and what remained was the voice underneath it, the one she did not use because using it meant there were no walls left. \\\"This is not the bond. This is not necessity.\\\"\", \"old_str\": \"Two paces. Close enough to see the attention in Lirien's eyes — not the color, because the dark had taken color, but the focus. The way Lirien held Kaelith's gaze with the steadiness of a person who had decided not to look away.\\n\\n\\\"This is not survival,\\\" Kaelith said. Her voice was low. Rough. The formality had stripped away and what remained was the voice underneath it, the one she did not use because using it meant there were no walls left. \\\"This is not the bond. This is not necessity.\\\"\", \"path\": \"/home/jakubs/repositories/books/romantasy-book-1/.romantic-fantasy-writer/stories/001/chapters/013/draft.md\"}"
